{
  "chapter": 2,
  "title": "Diacritics",
  "bookPages": "73-86",
  "sheet": {
    "kicker": "ARABIC101 / CHAPTER 02",
    "title": "Arabic Diacritics",
    "subtitle": "Tashkiil · The marks that tell you how to read each consonant",
    "coreIdeaLabel": "THE CORE IDEA",
    "coreIdea": "A diacritic tells you what sound comes **AFTER** the consonant. Learn the 4 harakaat, 3 tanween endings, and shaddah.",
    "coreIdeaNote": "Read Arabic right -> left. The letter and its mark work as one sound unit.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "1. Harakaat · three short vowels + sukuun",
        "bookPages": "73-75",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "cards",
            "columns": 4,
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "FATḤAH",
                "ar": "بَ",
                "translit": "ba",
                "caption": "One stroke **above**. Say a short /a/ after /b/."
              },
              {
                "title": "ḌAMMAH",
                "ar": "بُ",
                "translit": "bu",
                "caption": "Small curl **above**. Say a short /u/ after /b/."
              },
              {
                "title": "KASRAH",
                "ar": "بِ",
                "translit": "bi",
                "caption": "One stroke **below**. Say a short /i/ after /b/."
              },
              {
                "title": "SUKUUN",
                "ar": "بْ",
                "translit": "b",
                "caption": "Small circle **above**. No vowel: a pure consonant."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "tip",
            "title": "Put the sound after its letter.",
            "text": "كَتَبَ is **ka ta ba** (كَ ka · تَ ta · بَ ba) -- not ak at ab. A sukuun makes a consonant close its syllable: يَفْتَحُ = **yaf ta ḥu**."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "2. Tanween · a final \"n\" sound",
        "bookPages": "76",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "cards",
            "columns": 3,
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "TANWEEN ḌAMMAH",
                "ar": "بَيْتٌ",
                "translit": "bay tun",
                "caption": "Final sound = **-un**"
              },
              {
                "title": "TANWEEN KASRAH",
                "ar": "بَيْتٍ",
                "translit": "bay tin",
                "caption": "Final sound = **-in**"
              },
              {
                "title": "TANWEEN FATḤAH",
                "ar": "بَيْتًا",
                "translit": "bay tan",
                "caption": "Final sound = **-an**"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "tip",
            "title": "Spot the ending:",
            "text": "tanween is used only at the **end of a noun**, never at its beginning or middle. Tanween fatḥah adds a written ا after the final letter (alif tanween); **Chapter 4** covers spelling exceptions. These are full readings (waṣl); Chapter 5 explains stopping."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "3. Shaddah · double the consonant, not the vowel",
        "bookPages": "77-78",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "cards",
            "columns": 2,
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "What shaddah means",
                "ar": "بَّ",
                "caption": "= بْ b (no vowel) + بَ ba -> **bba**",
                "note": "The first copy of the letter has **no vowel**; the second copy takes the vowel mark. Pronounce them as one held consonant."
              },
              {
                "title": "Worked reading",
                "ar": "كَسَّرَ",
                "translit": "kas sa ra",
                "caption": "كَسْـ kas + سَرَ sa ra",
                "note": "A doubled **s**, not a long /a/."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "Do not miss:",
            "text": "shaddah always comes with **fatḥah, ḍammah, kasrah or tanween** -- never sukuun. Kasrah may appear below the letter or directly below the shaddah (يُسَبِّحُ yu sab bi ḥu); the pronunciation is identical. No word may begin with a sukuun."
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "exercisePages": "79-86",
    "exerciseTitle": "Use it on the exercises.",
    "exerciseIntro": "The answer comes from the **given sounds or visible marks**. You are learning to read and write tashkiil -- not yet deciding grammatical cases.",
    "exerciseTips": [
      {
        "title": "Read the task",
        "text": "Add marks? Match sounds? Transcribe?"
      },
      {
        "title": "Break into sounds",
        "text": "Vowel = **a / u / i**; no vowel = sukuun."
      },
      {
        "title": "Catch extras",
        "text": "Tanween = **-an / -un / -in**; double sound = shaddah."
      }
    ],
    "exerciseGuide": [
      {
        "exercise": 1,
        "pages": "79",
        "task": "Add fatḥah, kasrah, ḍammah",
        "method": "Keep the given letters in order. Read each Latin syllable: **ka** -> كَ, **tu** -> تُ, **bi** -> بِ. Put each short vowel over/under its own consonant. A consonant with no vowel after it gets sukuun.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "كَتَبَ",
          "translit": "ka ta ba",
          "note": "ka = كَ · ta = تَ · ba = بَ"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "دَرْسَ",
          "translit": "dar sa",
          "note": "Printed (da r sa): r has no vowel -> رْ"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 2,
        "pages": "80",
        "task": "Match Arabic words with Latin readings",
        "method": "Read each letter **right to left**, checking the difference between بَ, بُ, بِ and بْ. The options look similar: one changed mark changes the answer.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "كَتَبْتِ",
          "translit": "ka tab ti",
          "note": "MATCH d · بْ has no vowel; تِ adds -ti"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "كَتَبْتَ",
          "translit": "ka tab ta",
          "note": "MATCH a · final fatḥah, not kasrah"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 3,
        "pages": "81",
        "task": "Add ALL needed diacritics",
        "method": "Do not stop after adding short vowels. Check the transcription for **sukuun**, a repeated consonant (**shaddah**) and final **-n** (tanween). Final -an also needs a written ا.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "مُحَمَّدًا",
          "translit": "mu ḥam ma dan",
          "note": "مَّ = doubled m; final ـًا = -an"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "قِطٌّ",
          "translit": "qiṭ ṭun",
          "note": "Shaddah + tanween ḍammah on the last letter"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 4,
        "pages": "82",
        "task": "Match the Arabic word to its transcription",
        "method": "Work from the **written marks to sounds**. Check every vowel and the final ending before picking the lettered match, not just the consonants.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "دَرَسَ",
          "translit": "da ra sa",
          "note": "MATCH f (group B) · three fatḥahs = three short a sounds"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "دَرْسٌ",
          "translit": "dar sun",
          "note": "MATCH h · sukuun + tanween ḍammah"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": "Drills",
        "pages": "83-86",
        "task": "Write the letters first",
        "method": "The book recommends completing each word's letter shapes first, **then** placing the diacritics. Re-copy each drill carefully; do not interrupt the letter strokes to add marks.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "مُحَمَّدٌ",
          "translit": "mu ḥam ma dun",
          "note": "Step 1 · letters محمد  ->  Step 2 · add marks"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "ثَبَّتَ",
          "translit": "thab ba ta",
          "note": "Read it back: shaddah + fatḥah on ب"
        }
      }
    ],
    "exerciseFooter": "**Important for this workbook:** a letter with no mark may also indicate sukuun in the author's examples (p. 75: يَنسَخُ = يَنْسَخُ). But in ordinary unvowelled Arabic, missing marks do **not** automatically mean sukuun; the marks may simply be omitted."
  },
